        "math_academy_id": "q-110272",
        "problem": "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{\u2032}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(4)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |",
        "worked_solution": "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{\u2032}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(4)=\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{\u2032}(4){f}^{-1}(4).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=3,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{\u2032}(4)=5,$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(3)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{\u2032}(4) & =\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(4))}+{g}^{\u2032}(4){f}^{-1}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{\u2032}(3)}+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2}+15 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
